{
  "case_id": "2009-08-538",
  "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0000-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES: CRAIG G. GOLDBLATT, ESQ., Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the Petitioner. JEFFREY B. WALL, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor General, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on behalf of the United States, as amicus curiae, supporting the Petitioner. G. ERIC BRUNSTAD, JR., ESQ., Hartford, Conn.; on behalf of the Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Craig G. Goldblatt)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GOLDBLATT: Thank you. Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The debtor in this case claimed, in the third column of Schedule C, a $10,718 exempt interest in her kitchen equipment. That claim of exemption was fully proper. The trustee did not object to it because it was unobjectionable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Craig G. Goldblatt)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. GOLDBLATT: With respect, Justice Ginsburg, that's incorrect. If you turn to her Schedule C, which is in the joint appendix at pages 57 and 58a, the first column is a description of the property, and the third column contains the value of the claimed exemption."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Where would she say that, 100 percent interest in the equipment? Would she say that in -- in column 3?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Has that happened in the two circuits that apply a rule similar to this, the Sixth and the Third?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (as amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0001-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The government is not saying that it's a coincidence that these numbers in the third and fourth columns are the same. It is a common practice. A debtor will often estimate what she believes to be the market value of her property and then divvy up a wild card across items in hope -- hopes of keeping them. The government's and Petitioner's only point is that where a debtor does that, as Respondent did here, she's still claiming the fixed exemption of what she believes to be the market value."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (as amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0001-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That's right. And on remand, it would certainly be open to her to attempt to convince the Bankruptcy Court again that she had shown cause under 707 to dismiss. I think the government's point is that there is a process for sale. So even beyond the facts of this case, when the trustee wants to sell property, he has to give 20 days' notice to the debtors and the creditors under section 363 of the code and Rule 2002."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Is it also true \u2013- tell me about this: One of my concerns is that the trustees simply don't have time in every case to have a creditors\u2019 meeting and go through every asset."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "BUT HOW DO YOU KNOW THAT FROM THE FORM? Number one, I think both sides have -- have an argument as to what the form means. I don't think it's at all clear-cut. As I say, I'm looking for some kind of a rule to tilt the case one way or the other. All right? I don't put a lot of credence in the fact that she -- the ambiguities are construed against her."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Brunstad",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Actually, I think, Justice Kennedy, the opposite. After Taylor, after this Court's decision in Taylor, trustees understood if they had a valuation objection, if they had concern that the debtor might be getting a windfall, they needed to make an objection."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do you have any sense of how it works in practice? I'm a little worried by Justice Kennedy's question, because the government says in practice what's been happening is that in most places, trustees don't -- they don't object to these kinds of valuations problems, and now suddenly when the rule has changed in some circuit, they do object as a matter of form, which is unnecessary paperwork."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Brunstad",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If the trustee has any basis for objection, including valuation, and -- but the trustee has to have a good faith reason for objecting, and how that is determined is the trustee looks at the schedules, asks questions at the meeting of creditors, a section 341 meeting, and then if the trustee has any objection at all, present it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0003-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Now, it's important also because the practice, in bankruptcy, as reflected in the Collier forms, is that the bankruptcy court can make a judicial determination. Say, for example, the bankruptcy court here had said: I think there is some merit to the trustee's objection; the property is worth $12,000. The practice, as reflected in the sample form, is for the court then to say to the debtor: Debtor, if you want to keep this property, give the trustee a check for the difference between what you're entitled to claim and what I'm establishing the value to be."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Goldblatt",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Brunstad's suggestion that, historically, that there was -- the deadline applied to the work of liquidating the estate is simply incorrect."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "question, you asked, where do I turn to find out how hard it is to simply extend the deadline?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-538",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5f4fc8985c68b766cd30441856299f0693698f9f5a1326644617a5feb78f4b87",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5f4fc8985c68-0003-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 61,
        "start": 61
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
